>
  <p>
    Issuing a single surety bond rarely means producing a single document. For most
    court and probate bonds, it means producing four, each with overlapping data, each
    in a different format, and each entered by hand. Understanding the stack is the first
    step to understanding why the work takes thirty minutes that it should not.
  </p>

  <p>
    A surety bond is a three-party agreement. The <strong>principal</strong> is the party
    required to obtain the bond. The <strong>obligee</strong> is the party requiring it,
    often a court or government body. The <strong>surety</strong> is the carrier that
    guarantees the principal's obligation. The agency sits in the middle, turning a
    request into an executed, filed instrument. Here is what that takes.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The bond form</h2>

  <p>
    This is the instrument itself: the document that states the principal, the obligee,
    the penal sum, the conditions, and the effective date. The form is usually dictated
    by the obligee or the carrier, which means it is not yours to redesign. For court
    bonds, the required form often varies by jurisdiction, so the same guardianship bond
    can take a different shape in two adjacent counties.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The power of attorney</h2>

  <p>
    The power of attorney, or POA, is what gives the agency the authority to execute the
    bond on the carrier's behalf. It names the attorney-in-fact and the limit of their
    authority. A bond filed without a valid, current POA attached is not complete, and
    an obligee can reject it on that basis alone. Every executed bond needs its matching
    POA, and the limits have to line up with the penal sum.
  </p>

  <h2>3. The indemnity agreement</h2>

  <p>
    The general indemnity agreement, or GIA, is the contract in which the principal (and
    often individual indemnitors) agrees to make the surety whole for any loss. It is the
    document that protects the carrier, and by extension the agency's relationship with
    the carrier. It carries the most signatures and the most personal information, which
    is exactly why retyping it by hand is both the slowest step and the easiest to get
    wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The court cover sheet</h2>

  <p>
    For court and probate bonds, the bond does not stand alone. It is filed with a
    county-specific cover or filing sheet that routes it to the right division and case.
    Get the wrong sheet, or the wrong case number, and the filing bounces, no matter how
    perfect the bond underneath it is. This is the document most likely to be treated as
    an afterthought and most likely to cause a delay.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the same data gets typed four times</h2>

  <p>
    Look across the four and the problem is obvious. The principal's name, the bond
    amount, the obligee, the county, the effective date: the same handful of fields
    appear on every document, in different positions and different formats. The
    traditional workflow is to open four templates and key the same data into each, then
    proof each for the typo that will cause a rejection.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    The data is identical. The forms are not. That mismatch is the entire reason a bond
    takes half an hour instead of a minute.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    This is the specific problem SuretyOS is built to remove. One structured intake
    captures the shared data once. The bond form, power of attorney, indemnity
    agreement, and the correct county cover sheet come out pre-filled and consistent, and
    the agency's job shifts from data entry to review and approval. The stack does not get
    smaller. It just stops being typed by hand.
  </p>
